course: dictionary
lesson: 13071
title: Ferrous and non-ferrous metals
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Ferrous is a science word, and it is an adjective. It describes a metal that contains iron. Look at the clue in the word: if a metal has iron in it, scientists call it ferrous. «Steel bars are ferrous metals.» Steel is made with iron, so steel is ferrous.
explain: Ferrous metals usually rust and are magnetic, so a magnet will often pick them up. Tap your magnet on a steel spoon and then on a copper coin to test this. The spoon sticks to the magnet, but the coin does not.
explain: The prefix non- means not, so non-ferrous means not containing iron. Copper, aluminium and gold are non-ferrous metals. A quick check: swap your meaning into the sentence. «Copper is a non-ferrous metal» makes sense only if copper contains no iron.
fact: Ferrous means containing iron.
fact: Steel and iron are ferrous metals, and they usually rust and are magnetic.
fact: Copper, aluminium and gold are non-ferrous metals because they do not contain iron.
fact: Non- means not, so non-ferrous means not containing iron.
fact: Recycling centres use magnets to sort ferrous and non-ferrous metal.
exercise: «The old garden gate was made of ferrous metal.» What does ferrous mean here? | Painted bright red | Made of plastic | *Containing iron | Very shiny and gold
exercise: Which of these metals is non-ferrous? | *Aluminium | Iron gate | Iron nail | Steel
exercise: A recycling centre uses a magnet to sort scrap metal. Which metal will the magnet pick up? | Aluminium | *Steel | Copper | Gold
test: «The ferrous pipes in the old house had gone rusty.» What does ferrous mean? | Light in weight | *Containing iron | Hollow | Shiny
test: Which word means not containing iron? | Ferrous | Rusty | Magnetic | *Non-ferrous
exam: Which metal is ferrous? | Aluminium | Copper | *Steel | Gold
